\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 JD Vance\u8217\'20\'19s debate lines were so polished you could forget they made no sense\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 967 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par 11:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s running mate had the greasy self-assurance of someone used to lying to people he thinks are stupid\par \par \uc2 Maybe he thought the pink tie could help. JD Vance, the Ohio senator and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s running mate, clearly set out to make himself seem less creepy at Tuesday night\u8217\'20\'19s vice-presidential debate, and a major target of this project was aimed at convincing women voters to like him. Vance, after all, has what pollsters call \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/favorability/jd-vance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high unfavorables}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, which is a polite way of saying that people hate his guts.\par \par \uc2 Much of this stems from Vance\u8217\'20\'19s extreme and inflexible views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , his hostility to childless women, and his creepy statements about families and childrearing. He had to convince women that he\u8217\'20\'19s not out to hurt them or monitor their menstrual cycles; he had to try and seem kindly, empathetic, gentle. The resulting 90 minutes felt like watching a remarkably lifelike robot try to imitate normal human emotion. He smiled. He cooed. He spoke of an anonymous woman he knew whom he said was watching, and told her: \u8220\'20\'1CLove ya\u8221\'20\'1D. And occasionally, when he was fact-checked or received pushback on his falsehoods or distortions, the eyes of his stiff, fixed face flashed with an incandescent rage.\par \par \uc2 A generous characterization of Vance\u8217\'20\'19s performance might be to call it \u8220\'20\'1Cslick\u8221\'20\'1D. Vance delivered practiced answers to questions on healthcare, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and childcare that were dense with lies and euphemism. Asked about his call for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Vance insisted that what he wanted was a national \u8220\'20\'1Cstandard\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a standard, that is, to ban it at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 He spoke in what was probably supposed to be empathetic terms about a woman he had grown up with who had told him that she felt she had had to have the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  she got when they were younger, because it allowed her to leave her abusive relationship \u8211\'20\'13 without clarifying that the laws that Vance supports would have compelled that woman he purports to care about to carry her abuser\u8217\'20\'19s child to term, and likely become trapped with him.\par \par \uc2 He claimed that Americans didn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Ctrust\u8221\'20\'1D Republicans on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, but did not mention that they don\u8217\'20\'19t trust Republicans because those are the ones taking their rights away.\par \par \uc2 When asked about childcare, Vance spoke in eerily imprecise terms about encouraging people to choose their preferred \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily model\u8221\'20\'1D, without specifying exactly which \u8220\'20\'1Cmodel\u8221\'20\'1D he had in mind. He spoke of the \u8220\'20\'1Cmultiple people who could be providing family care options\u8221\'20\'1D but did not specify if these \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople\u8221\'20\'1D had anything in common with each other. In media appearances throughout his career, Vance has been more explicit: he means that women will perform childcare for free \u8211\'20\'13 dropping out of paid work in the public sphere to do so, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Vance was confident and smiling as he delivered these lines; he had the greasy self-assurance of someone who is used to lying to people he thinks are stupider than him. He sounded every bit like the Yale Law lawyer that he is. Even when he was not degrading women\u8217\'20\'19s dignity or condescending to the two female moderators, his answers were often delivered with a polish that seemed intended to conceal the fact that they made no sense.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the housing crisis, for instance, he said that mass deportations \u8211\'20\'13 a horrific ethnic-cleansing operation proposed by the Trump campaign that would ruin communities, families and lives \u8211\'20\'13 would lower prices by decreasing demand. It was a kind of repeat of Jonathan Swift\u8217\'20\'19s A Modest Proposal, but this time it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t satire. He also suggested that the government could build housing on federal lands \u8211\'20\'13 but neglected to mention that most of those lands are in the vast, rural, empty Mountain West, in regions with lots of tumbleweeds and absolutely no jobs.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps Vance\u8217\'20\'19s quintessential moment of the night came early, when he was attempting to further his smears of the Haitian immigrant community of Springfield, Ohio, whom he had previously targeted with lies that they eat pets. Vance was cut off by the moderators, but talked over them insistently. \u8220\'20\'1CMargaret. Margaret. Margaret. Margaret\u8221\'20\'1D, he said repeatedly, trying to strong-arm one of the women into letting him talk. As they corrected his misstatements, he whined to the women: \u8220\'20\'1CThe rules were that you guys weren\u8217\'20\'19t going to fact-check!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , the Minnesota governor, reportedly told \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  when he was being vetted for the job that he does not consider himself a good debater. Going in, expectations for him were low. And indeed Walz had an uneven night, sometimes appearing flustered or nervous. An early answer on foreign policy, in particular, was confused and unconvincing. But Walz visibly gained confidence throughout the debate, issuing more forceful answers, attacking Trump and Vance\u8217\'20\'19s record, and emphasizing himself, often successfully, as a homespun purveyor of goodwill and common sense.\par \par \uc2 He was most convincing on what seems to be, for him, the most morally animating issues: healthcare and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Walz named Amber Thurman, a woman killed by an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, as someone whose life could have been saved were it not for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s policies; he spoke with passion and clarity of how Trump\u8217\'20\'19s plan to reverse the Affordable Care Act would kick millions off their insurance.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps Walz\u8217\'20\'19s best moment came near the end of the debate, in a conversation about democracy, when he pointedly asked JD Vance to say whether Trump lost the 2020 election. Vance dodged.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a damning non-answer,\u8221\'20\'1D Walz said. It could have summarized Vance\u8217\'20\'19s whole performance.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241002eka2001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
