\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  accused of \u8216\'20\'18intimidation campaign\u8217\'20\'19 against  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Joseph Contreras in Miami \par \uc2 1,149 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2024\par 15:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Florida voters report law enforcement personnel visits that appear to be part of drive to block passage of Amendment 4\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is making a concerted effort to maintain draconian limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Florida that have led to accusations the rightwing Republican governor is conducting a \u8220\'20\'1Cstate-sponsored intimidation campaign\u8221\'20\'1D against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and trampling on civil liberties in the state.\par \par \uc2 A near total ban on abortions after the first six weeks of pregnancy took effect in Florida in May after the state supreme court ruled that the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was no longer covered by the privacy clause in the Florida constitution.\par \par \uc2 Passage of legislation called Amendment 4 would change the state constitution to prohibit government interference with the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the viability of a fetus, which typically begins around the 24th week of a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But registered voters in Florida have recently reported unannounced visits from law enforcement personnel that appear to be part of an all-out drive by DeSantis to use state government agencies and public funds to block passage of Amendment 4, which would enshrine in the state constitution a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The experience of Isaac Menasche is a cautionary tale. In early September, Menasche received an unexpected visitor at his home in the Florida Gulf coast city of Fort Myers \u8211\'20\'13 a plainclothes detective with a badge and a folder stuffed with documents containing Menasche\u8217\'20\'19s personal information.\par \par \uc2 They included copies of his driver\u8217\'20\'19s license and a petition form he had signed months ago at a local farmer\u8217\'20\'19s market on behalf of a campaign to qualify a pro-choice referendum for the statewide ballot in this year\u8217\'20\'19s general election.\par \par \uc2 The detective who turned up on Menasche\u8217\'20\'19s doorstep wanted to know why his signature on the petition form did not match the one on his driver\u8217\'20\'19s license. The retired 71-year-old attorney conceded the point but explained that his signature can sometimes vary. The officer left shortly thereafter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe experience left me shaken,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the New Jersey native on his \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page that same day. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was obvious to me that a significant effort was exerted to determine if indeed I had signed the petition. Troubling that so much resources were devoted to this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 DeSantis initially asked the Florida supreme court to declare the ballot measure unconstitutional on the grounds that its language was vague and misleading. When that ploy failed last April, DeSantis shifted gears: in July a senior official in the state government department in charge of elections announced a review for possible fraud of tens of thousands of petition signatures collected in four counties in support of Amendment 4.\par \par \uc2 In more recent weeks, the state-run \uc2 Agency for Health Care Administration\uc2  (\uc2 AHCA\uc2 ) has launched a website opposing the initiative on the grounds that it \u8220\'20\'1Cthreatens women\u8217\'20\'19s safety\u8221\'20\'1D. It has also spent millions of dollars on television ads urging Florida voters to reject the proposed amendment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re seeing a state-sponsored intimidation campaign to make Floridians scared of voicing support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,\u8221\'20\'1D says Keisha Mulfort, a spokesperson for the American Civil Liberties Union of Florida which filed a lawsuit earlier this month seeking to halt the \uc2 AHCA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s anti-Amendment 4 media campaign.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFlorida\u8217\'20\'19s leadership has made it clear they don\u8217\'20\'19t trust women to make decisions about their own healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ll go to great lengths to demonstrate they don\u8217\'20\'19t support the democratic process, including sending law enforcement personnel to the homes of private citizens.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 DeSantis recently defended the state-funded anti-amendment website and television ads as \u8220\'20\'1Cpublic service announcements\u8221\'20\'1D similar to those produced by the Florida department of transportation to encourage safe driving.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s being used by the \uc2 AHCA\uc2  agency to basically provide people with accurate information,\u8221\'20\'1D said the governor during a roundtable discussion held in a Miami suburb on 9 September. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything that is put out is factual. That\u8217\'20\'19s been done for decades, it\u8217\'20\'19s not electioneering, and it is not inappropriate at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AHCA\uc2  communications office failed to respond to a list of written questions submitted by the Guardian about the agency\u8217\'20\'19s website and electronic media campaign. The governor\u8217\'20\'19s communications director, Bryan Griffin, turned down \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s request for an interview with DeSantis, asserting that the newspaper was \u8220\'20\'1Ccompletely consumed with left wing activism and does nothing to actually inform the public.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Under a law passed by the Republican-dominated Florida legislature, ballot measures must be approved by 60% of the electorate, and Amendment 4 proponents say they are confident of meeting that threshold in the general election scheduled for 5 November.\par \par \uc2 To bolster his case against the pro-choice amendment proposal, DeSantis has even questioned the legitimacy of passing laws through popular referendums, even though that mechanism is authorized by the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt takes power away from the people to be able to decide this through elections and who they elect to office and who legislates,\u8221\'20\'1D he told a press conference recently. \u8220\'20\'1CIt effectively puts it in the courts, and there will be 25 years\u8217\'20\'19 worth of lawsuits on what any of these terms mean.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a letter dated 25 January of this year, the Florida department of state\u8217\'20\'19s division of elections confirmed that the six organizations in support of the pro-choice referendum had collected enough valid signatures to qualify the proposed constitutional amendment for the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 Six months later, however, a deputy secretary of that same state government department revealed in a letter that his office had received \u8220\'20\'1Calarming information\u8221\'20\'1D from the Palm Beach county supervisor of elections office about \u8220\'20\'1Cfraudulent constitutional initiative petitions\u8221\'20\'1D that were submitted by 35 individuals who had been hired to collect signatures on behalf of Amendment 4.\par \par \uc2 This apparent attempt to reopen the signature validity issue was replicated in three other counties in Florida, and as of two weeks ago an estimated 36,000 signatures are currently under review by an election fraud unit that was established by legislation that DeSantis signed into law two years ago.\par \par \uc2 The Palm Beach county supervisor of elections, Wendy Sartory Link, received an email four weeks ago from that deputy secretary of state, Brad McVay, asking her office to review 17,637 petition forms that were certified as valid by her office last winter.\par \par \uc2 The elections supervisor said the request from McVay was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a common practice\u8221\'20\'1D that she had encountered in the five years since she was appointed to the position by DeSantis. Link is running for re-election this year as a Democrat, and she suggested that the entire exercise might be an academic one at this juncture.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really apply to us,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe initiative was certified, and it\u8217\'20\'19s on our ballots.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240929ek9t001p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
