\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Where will  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  be on the ballot in the 2024 US election?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,171 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2024\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ten states will vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November, with most seeking to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until fetal viability\par \par \uc2 This November, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be on the ballot in 10 states, including the states that could determine the next president.\par \par \uc2 In the two years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become the kind of issue that decides elections. Outrage over Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise led Republicans to flounder in the 2022 midterms, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have won every post-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measure, including in red states such as Ohio, Kentucky and Kansas.\par \par \uc2 This year, most of the ballot measures are seeking to amend states\u8217\'20\'19 constitutions to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights up until fetal viability, or about 24 weeks of pregnancy. Because a number of the measures are in states that have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlawed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , they could become the first to overturn the post-Roe ban. Others are in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, but activists say the measures are necessary to cement protections so they can\u8217\'20\'19t be easily overturned if Republicans control the government.\par \par \uc2 These are the states slated to vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this election day.\par \par \uc2 Arizona\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters in Arizona, a key battleground state in the presidential election, are vying to pass a measure that would enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until viability in the state constitution. A provider could perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability if the procedure is necessary to protect the life or physical or mental health of a patient.\par \par \uc2 Arizona currently bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 15 weeks of pregnancy. Earlier this year, the state supreme court reinstated a 19th-century near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, generating nationwide outrage that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/02/arizona-1864-abortion-ban-repealed-governor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prompted the state legislature}}}\uc2  to quickly repeal it in favor of letting the 15-week ban stand.\par \par \uc2 Colorado\par \par \uc2 Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s measure would amend the state constitution to block the state government from denying, impeding or discriminating against individuals\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cright to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. This measure also includes a one-of-a-kind provision to bar Colorado from prohibiting healthcare coverage for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which could very well pass in the deep-blue state.\par \par \uc2 Because Colorado permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy and neighbors five states with bans \u8211\'20\'13 Oklahoma, Texas, Arizona, Utah and Nebraska \u8211\'20\'13 the state has become a haven for people fleeing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, especially those seeking abortions later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Florida\par \par \uc2 Once the last stronghold of southern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, Florida in May {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/03/florida-abortion-ban-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banned \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  past six weeks of pregnancy}}}\uc2 , which is before many women know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant. Its measure, which needs 60% of the vote to pass, would roll back that ban by adding the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until viability to the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. Providers could perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability if one is needed to protect a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health.\par \par \uc2 Florida Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 tactics in the fight against the measure has alarmed voting rights and civil rights groups. Law enforcement officials have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/13/florida-abortion-petition-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigated voters who signed petitions}}}\uc2  to get the measure onto the ballot, while a state health agency has created a webpage attacking the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Maryland\par \par \uc2 Legislators, rather than citizens, initiated Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s measure, which would amend the state constitution to confirm individuals\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cright to reproductive freedom, including but not limited to the ability to make and effectuate decisions to prevent, continue, or end the individual\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D. Like Colorado, Maryland has become an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven because it permits the procedure throughout pregnancy. It is also relatively close to the deep south, which is blanketed in bans.\par \par \uc2 Missouri\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/sep/10/missouri-supreme-court-abortion-rights-ballot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  opponents went to court}}}\uc2  to stop Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s measure from appearing on voters\u8217\'20\'19 ballots, but the state supreme court rejected their arguments and agreed to let voters decide whether the Missouri constitution should guarantee the \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamental right to reproductive freedom, which is the right to make and carry out decisions about all matters relating to reproductive healthcare, including but not limited to prenatal care, childbirth, postpartum care, birth control, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, miscarriage care, and respectful birthing conditions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Missouri, which was the first state to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Roe fell, only permits the procedure in medical emergencies. If the measure passes, it is expected to roll back that ban and permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability.\par \par \uc2 Montana\par \par \uc2 In the years since Roe fell, Montana courts and its Republican-dominated legislature have wrestled over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and whether the right to privacy embedded in Montana\u8217\'20\'19s constitution includes the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal until viability in Montana, but the measure would amend the state constitution to explicitly include \u8220\'20\'1Ca right to make and carry out decisions about one\u8217\'20\'19s own pregnancy, including the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D up until viability. Providers could perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability to protect a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life or health.\par \par \uc2 Nebraska\par \par \uc2 Nebraska, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 12 weeks of pregnancy, is the lone state with two competing ballot measures this November. One of the measures would enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until viability into the state constitution, while the other would enshrine the current ban. If both measures pass, the measure that garners the most votes would take effect.\par \par \uc2 Nevada\par \par \uc2 Alongside Arizona, Nevada is one of the most closely watched states in the presidential election. Its measure would amend the state constitution to protect individuals\u8217\'20\'19 right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until viability, or after viability in cases where a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health or life may be threatened. Nevada already permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until 26 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 New York\par \par \uc2 New York state legislators added a measure to the ballot to broaden the state\u8217\'20\'19s anti-discrimination laws by adding, among other things, protections against discrimination on the basis of \u8220\'20\'1Csex, including sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy outcomes, and reproductive health\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Although sky-blue New York passed a law protecting reproductive rights in 2019, advocates say this measure could be used to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights against future challenges. However, the ballot language before voters will not include the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, leading advocates to fear voters will not understand what they are voting on. Democrats pushed to add the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D to the description of the measure, but a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-new-york-election-2024-ad674ee66389e3703a847fa385a308c7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 judge rejected the request}}}\uc2 , ruling that the amendment poses \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplex interpretive questions\u8221\'20\'1D and its exact impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is unclear.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota\par \par \uc2 South Dakota\u8217\'20\'19s measure is less sweeping than other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures, because it would only protect the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first trimester of pregnancy. Under this measure, South Dakota could regulate access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Conly in ways that are reasonably related to the physical health of the pregnant woman\u8221\'20\'1D in the second trimester of pregnancy. In the third trimester, the state could ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in medical emergencies. Right now, South Dakota only allows abortions in such emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Although this measure will appear on the ballot, there will be a trial over the validity of the signatures that were collected for it. Depending out the outcome of the trial, the measure \u8211\'20\'13 and any votes cast for it \u8211\'20\'13 could be invalidated.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240926ek9q001p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
