\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18One death is too many\u8217\'20\'19:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans usher in US maternal mortality crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2024\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Recent studies highlight threats to health of women and infants as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions change US health landscape\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, doctors will no longer be able to carry a lifesaving medication with them during pregnancy emergencies. In Texas, the infant mortality rate is soaring. In Idaho, pregnant people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/03/21/idaho-hospital-baby-delivery-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drive hours}}}\uc2  just to give birth. And in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2023/04/25/1171851775/oklahoma-woman-abortion-ban-study-shows-confusion-at-hospitals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma}}}\uc2  and Georgia, women are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/09/john-mcentee-trump-tiktok-bleeding-out-abortion-bans-harris-debate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bleeding out}}}\uc2  in hospital parking lots and facing dangerous infections before they can find care \u8211\'20\'13 and sometimes, that care comes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 too late}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The limitations and outright bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have taken hold in half of the US in the wake of the Dobbs decision have wreaked enormous changes to the reproductive health landscape.\par \par \uc2 The restrictions put a growing burden on the health and wellbeing of patients and providers, even as more Americans find it difficult to find and access care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe United States is, and has been for quite some time, in the midst of a maternal and infant mortality crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Jamila Perritt, a board-certified obstetrician and gynecologist and president of Physicians for Reproductive Health.\par \par \uc2 Banning access to reproductive healthcare, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, is \u8220\'20\'1Cdirectly causing an increase in morbidity and mortality in our community\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have really robust evidence that shows us that when people have sought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and are unable to obtain it, their psychological, social, physical and emotional health is harmed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Maternal and infant mortality will probably increase because of the restrictions \u8211\'20\'13 especially if national limitations, like enforcement of the Comstock Act, are put into place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI expect in the next few years, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to start to see the infant mortality, pre-term birth, maternal mortality, and maternal morbidity numbers rise for everyone, and particularly for folks from racially marginalized and low-socioeconomic communities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel Hardeman, professor of reproductive health and founding director of the Center for Antiracism Research for Health Equity at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Calculating mortality is often a complicated and controversial endeavor. When maternal mortality dropped in 2022, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/23/23-726/301607/20240227161735866_23-726%2023-727%20Brief.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 credited}}}\uc2  the success to the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 But that\u8217\'20\'19s not the case, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2822873"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new research}}}\uc2  in Jama Network Open.\par \par \uc2 Maternal deaths surged during the first two years of the pandemic, when Covid, a deadly illness during pregnancy, accounted for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-23-105871.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one-quarter }}}\uc2  of all maternal deaths. But in 2022, that rate dropped to levels similar to pre-pandemic levels, from 32.9 deaths per 100,000 live births in 2021 to 22.3 in 2022.\par \par \uc2 That rate is still higher than maternal mortality rates in peer nations, and research indicates it will increase if officials clamp down on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally \u8211\'20\'13 which, with the Comstock Act, would require no additional anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation or bans.\par \par \uc2 Right now, \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople can still travel to other states, but once there\u8217\'20\'19s a federal ban, that won\u8217\'20\'19t be an option any more,\u8221\'20\'1D says Amanda Jean Stevenson, co-author of the new Jama research and a sociologist at the \uc2 University of Colorado Boulder\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd there could be this very different set of outcomes when people\u8217\'20\'19s options are gone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the year following Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, child mortality shot up by 12.9% \u8211\'20\'13 compared with a 1.8% increase in the rest of the country\par \par \uc2 In the United States, more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2022/p0919-pregnancy-related-deaths.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 80%}}}\uc2  of pregnancy-related deaths and more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bmcpublichealth.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12889-022-14913-z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 60%}}}\uc2  of infant deaths in the first week are preventable \u8211\'20\'13 and those figures were estimated before the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 Because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, there are already significant challenges to accessing reproductive healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s not just \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, misoprostol \u8211\'20\'13 a drug used for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other lifesaving purposes \u8211\'20\'13 will be labeled a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lailluminator.com/2024/09/03/louisiana-women/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 controlled substance}}}\uc2  beginning on 1 October. One of its uses is keeping patients from bleeding out after childbirth, which is the No 1 cause of postpartum mortality.\par \par \uc2 Yet physicians cannot keep controlled substances in their emergency carts, and they fear they won\u8217\'20\'19t have enough time to fill prescriptions for patients when minutes and even seconds make the difference between life and death.\par \par \uc2 In the year following Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, child mortality shot up by 12.9% \u8211\'20\'13 compared with a 1.8% increase in the rest of the country, according to a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamapediatrics/article-abstract/2819785?os&ref=app"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2 . Congenital anomalies are the leading cause of infant death in the US \u8211\'20\'13 but while they went down by 3.1% in the rest of the country, they went up by 22.9% in Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat study was chilling. That is a huge change,\u8221\'20\'1D Stevenson said.\par \par \uc2 It echoed previous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajpmonline.org/article/S0749-3797(23)00408-7/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2  finding that states with the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws saw 16% more infant deaths between 2014 and 2018.\par \par \uc2 The trauma and costs of carrying to term pregnancies that are incompatible with life inflict an incalculable toll on families and providers.\par \par \uc2 States are closing obstetric units and losing experienced providers who worry about not being able to offer lifesaving care as patients die on the table in front of them, and facing jail time if they provide care.\par \par \uc2 More than two-thirds (68%) of obstetricians and gynecologists say the Dobbs decision has made it harder for them to respond to pregnancy-related emergencies, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/report/a-national-survey-of-obgyns-experiences-after-dobbs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to the non-partisan health research organization KFF. They also believe it has worsened mortality in pregnancy while increasing racial and ethnic inequities, and fewer doctors are now interested in entering the field.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, soon after the Dobbs decision, medical residency applications {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aamcresearchinstitute.org/our-work/data-snapshot/post-dobbs-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped}}}\uc2  for states with bans.\par \par \uc2 More than half of doctors surveyed in states with bans and limitations said they were very concerned about legal repercussions to providing the standard of care in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat can just exacerbate already pretty large gaps in the workforce,\u8221\'20\'1D said Usha Ranji, associate director for women\u8217\'20\'19s health policy at KFF.\par \par \uc2 In the past two years, more than 100 hospitals have closed their obstetric units entirely, according to a new March of Dimes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marchofdimes.org/maternity-care-deserts-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 . More than one-third of US counties are now maternity care deserts, with no obstetricians or places to give birth. North Dakota, South Dakota, Alaska, Oklahoma and Nebraska have the least access to maternity care.\par \par \uc2 The majority of rural hospitals ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ruralhospitals.chqpr.org/downloads/Rural_Maternity_Care_Crisis.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 57%}}}\uc2  ) no longer deliver babies, with more than 100 of the rural hospitals ending labor and delivery services in the past five years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve created policy and legislation to limit access to abortions and also have closed the exact places that people need to go to get care if they are pregnant,\u8221\'20\'1D Hardeman said. That puts pressure on neighboring states that still provide care, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere you live matters for your health. And I think that the Dobbs decision and the fall of Roe have demonstrated that in a very real and very obvious way, because there are literally places in this country where there is essentially no access to reproductive healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt started out in rural spaces, but more and more, there\u8217\'20\'19s reports popping up of labor and delivery units closing in, like, urban Chicago.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In states with restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women of color and pregnant people from low-income communities often suffer the most, said Hardeman. \u8220\'20\'1CTaking away access to reproductive healthcare is exacerbating those disparities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Black women die because of pregnancy at twice the national rate, and three times more than white women, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2021/maternal-mortality-rates-2021.htm#:~:text=In%202021%2C%201%2C205%20women%20died,20.1%20in%202019%20(Table)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to the \uc2 US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact that black women and birthing people are dying at three to four times greater risk than white women is shameful \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s preventable,\u8221\'20\'1D Perritt said.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s important to shore up protections for reproductive health for all Americans before health complications and mortality rise even more, she said. \u8220\'20\'1COne death is too much. One is too many.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240925ek9p001mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
