\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 More than 200 pregnancy-related prosecutions in first year post-Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2024\par 14:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Study finds highest yearly number of prosecutions related to pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 and experts say that is likely an undercount\par \par \uc2 In the year Roe v Wade was overturned, at least 200 people in the US were prosecuted for conduct relating to their pregnancies \u8211\'20\'13 the highest number of cases in a single year ever recorded, according to a new report released on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The report, compiled by the advocacy group Pregnancy Justice, is the first comprehensive accounting of pregnancy-related criminal charges between June 2022 and June 2023, but researchers warn that it is still probably an undercount.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo be perfectly honest, I think we\u8217\'20\'19re scratching the surface of what is happening,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wendy Bach, a \uc2 University of Tennessee\uc2  law professor and the report\u8217\'20\'19s principal investigator.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of prosecutions documented in the report do not involve abortions. However, five cases mention allegations of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or \u8220\'20\'1Cresearching or exploring the possibility of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, according to the report. Only one was charged under a statute meant to criminalize abortions. The rest involved a bevy of other laws, such as a statute that bans the \u8220\'20\'1Cabuse of a corpse\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Four of those cases took place in states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or are hostile to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 More than 200 of the 210 recorded prosecutions involve allegations of substance use during pregnancy. In almost 200 of the cases, prosecutors charged people using statutes that criminalize child abuse, neglect, or endangerment \u8211\'20\'13 charges that treat an embryo or fetus as a person, complete with rights and protections that may compete with that of the person carrying them. More than 100 prosecutions recorded by Pregnancy Justice took place in Alabama, a state whose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/21/alabama-ivf-embryo-extrauterine-children-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court recently ruled}}}\uc2  that embryos were \u8220\'20\'1Cextrauterine children\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Most of the cases also involved statutes under which prosecutors do not need to prove that any harm was done to a fetus or infant. Rather, prosecutors must show that a defendant posed some \u8220\'20\'1Crisk\u8221\'20\'1D to the pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 which could lead to criminalization of behavior that is not actually dangerous, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s ultimately, a lot of the time, based on someone\u8217\'20\'19s perceptions of risky behavior, however they might define it, and it\u8217\'20\'19s often based on stereotypes or outdated notions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Zenovia Earle, media and communications director for Pregnancy Justice.\par \par \uc2 People have faced criminal consequences over their pregnancies even before Roe fell. In just one example, Alabama police arrested Ashley Caswell, then two months pregnant, in 2021 for allegedly testing positive for methamphetamine and \u8220\'20\'1Cendangering\u8221\'20\'1D her fetus, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/13/alabama-pregnant-woman-jail-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Guardian reported last year}}}\uc2 . Jailed for the rest of her pregnancy, Caswell was forced to deliver her baby alone, in a jail shower, according to court and medical records.\par \par \uc2 Over three-quarters of the cases uncovered by Pregnancy Justice also involved defendants who are low-income.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-two of the cases involved fetal or infant demise. For Bach, this is evidence that, in a post-Roe country, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/jan/24/us-miscarriage-laws-abortion-rights-options"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnancy loss is suspect\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe idea that the fetus can be a person and a victim of a crime is being wielded in significant ways when there\u8217\'20\'19s a pregnancy loss,\u8221\'20\'1D Bach said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo rather than meeting a pregnancy loss with care, with support, with an acknowledgement for the often tragic life circumstances that that involves \u8211\'20\'13 we are meeting it with criminal suspicion, with criminal investigation and with prosecution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kffhealthnews.org/news/article/pregnancy-loss-criminalization-homicide-south-carolina-college-student/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 KFF Health News shared the story of}}}\uc2  a 23-year-old woman who miscarried in South Carolina, and was arrested in June 2023 and charged with \u8220\'20\'1Cmurder/homicide by child abuse\u8221\'20\'1D. The woman, Amari Marsh, was held in jail for 22 days, then was placed under house arrest, and had her case cleared by a grand jury in August \u8211\'20\'13 13 months after leaving jail. Marsh was facing the possibility of 20 years to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban was not in effect at the time of Marsh\u8217\'20\'19s arrest; today, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state is banned after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In another high-profile case, a grand jury declined to pursue the case of Brittany Watts, an Ohio woman who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/11/ohio-woman-miscarriage-not-indicted-abuse-corpse-brittany-watts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly miscarried into a toilet}}}\uc2  last year. A nurse reportedly told the police about Watts, who was charged under an \u8220\'20\'1Cabuse of a corpse\u8221\'20\'1D statute. Her case falls out of the range of the Pregnancy Justice report.\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy Justice found that more than half of the cases involved information obtained in a medical setting. In a separate 2023 study of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/30/self-managed-abortions-arrest-investiagtion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminalization of self-managed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  between 2000 and 2020, the reproductive justice group If/When/How found that in 45% of cases, it was healthcare providers or social workers who had tipped off police to a suspected self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a significant phenomenon, and now we have people all over the country hiding real healthcare needs and not reaching out for help. That\u8217\'20\'19s the effect of this,\u8221\'20\'1D Bach said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would like a society in which people who need care, seek care. But if this is what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen, then it is totally rational to not seek care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Pregnancy Justice, the link between prosecutions and the medical establishment raises questions about the post-Roe surveillance of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 in particular, how the CDC, which already tracks nationwide information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could expand its reach.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025, a playbook of policies for a future conservative administration drafted by the influential thinktank \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , suggests that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/05/project-2025-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CDC force every state}}}\uc2  to report the number of abortions it performs, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  complications, miscarriages, stillbirths and \u8220\'20\'1Ctreatments that incidentally result in the death of a child (such as chemotherapy)\u8221\'20\'1D. Bach and Earle declined to discuss the Project 2025 proposal, citing Pregnancy Justice\u8217\'20\'19s nonprofit status.\par \par \uc2 The findings released on Tuesday are preliminary; Pregnancy Justice researchers intend to conclude the study in two years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery day, as the research continues,\u8221\'20\'1D Bach said, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe discover more evidence of this and the harder we look, the more we find.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240924ek9o001s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
