\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Amber Thurman was killed by Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban. There will be others\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 907 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2024\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thurman, a 28-year-old mother, died because Georgia barred doctors from doing a simple procedure to save her life\par \par \uc2 There are other names, but this is the one we know: Amber Thurman has become the first woman whose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/17/harris-trump-georgia-abortion-death-amber-nicole-thurman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 death was preventable}}}\uc2  in relation to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs}}}\uc2 . Her name and story have become public as reporting by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Kavitha Surana {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 details}}}\uc2  how Thurman, a Black 28-year-old mother to a young son who had dreams of becoming a nurse, died a painful, preventable death in Georgia after doctors at a hospital there refused to perform a simple procedure that could have saved her life \u8211\'20\'13 because the law did not allow them.\par \par \uc2 The story highlights the reality of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2 , which \u8211\'20\'13 even in states like Georgia, with putative exceptions for maternal health \u8211\'20\'13 in practice impose death sentences on women who seek to end their pregnancies, or who experience severe complications. They force doctors to choose between medical best practices and their own legal protection \u8211\'20\'13 and in the process, the lives of women are treated as alarmingly disposable.\par \par \uc2 Thurman, who lived in Georgia, died just weeks after her state\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  ban went into effect. She had just established a new degree of stability for herself and her young son when she discovered that she was pregnant with twins in 2022. As her pregnancy had already progressed beyond her state\u8217\'20\'19s gestational limit, she took a road trip to North Carolina with her best friend, where a clinic gave her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have very low rates of complications but rare problems do occur. In Thurman\u8217\'20\'19s case, not all of the pregnancy tissue had been expelled from her uterus, and she arrived in a Georgia ER with bleeding, pain and falling blood pressure \u8211\'20\'13 the telltale signs of an infection.\par \par \uc2 Thurman could have been cured with a D&C, or dilation and curettage, a procedure in which the cervix is dilated to create an opening through which instruments can be inserted to empty out the contents of a uterus. The procedure is a popular form of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it is also a routine part of miscarriage and other gynecological care. If the tissue was promptly removed, she probably would have been fine: a D&C requires no special equipment and takes only about 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 But Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban outlawed the D&C procedure, making it a felony to perform except in cases of managing a \u8220\'20\'1Cspontaneous\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cnaturally occurring\u8221\'20\'1D miscarriage. Because Thurman had taken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, her miscarriage was illegal to treat. She suffered in a hospital bed for 20 hours, developing sepsis and beginning to experience organ failure. By the time the Georgia doctors were finally willing to treat her, it was too late.\par \par \uc2 We don\u8217\'20\'19t know how many other women have died because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Such cases are often shrouded in secrecy, stigma and confidentiality. And few families, let alone those grieving the loss of vibrant, loving young women, are willing to subject themselves to the smears and scrutiny that coming forward about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban deaths will inevitably entail.\par \par \uc2 But we will also not know because ban states, where these deaths are taking place, are not incentivized to reveal them. Thurman\u8217\'20\'19s death was ruled preventable by a state maternal mortality review board. But it was only \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reporting that made the cause of her death public. Not even her family were told that Amber could have been saved: the state did not bother to inform them, and they learned from \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reporter.\par \par \uc2 But even if we knew all the names of those who have been killed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, we would still not be able to fathom the full scope of what has been taken from us. Every life \u8211\'20\'13 every woman\u8217\'20\'19s life \u8211\'20\'13 is a world of possibility: these murders-by-neglect to which the law sentences women like Amber exterminate those worlds.\par \par \uc2 Amber will never live her dream of going to nursing school. Her best friend, Ricaria Baker, who drove her to the North Carolina clinic, will never laugh with her again. Her mother has lost a child. Her son, who was six when Thurman died, will grow up without his mother: she will never get to know the man he becomes, and he will never get to understand her as an adult.\par \par \uc2 All because the self-interest of cynical Republican politicians, the treacly, misogynist sentimentality of the anti-choice movement, and the myopic bigotry of six supreme court justices \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and John Roberts \u8211\'20\'13 were deemed more important than her life, her dreams, her ambitions and all the love her family had for her.\par \par \uc2 We do know that there almost certainly are other women like Thurman. We may well never know their stories, their names. But we know that we are worse off without them; we know that they deserved better; we know that their lives mattered \u8211\'20\'13 more than politics and more than other people\u8217\'20\'19s religion. We can pray for those who loved them, and we can fight for those that will come next. And we can hope, too, that Amber Thurman\u8217\'20\'19s face will haunt the nightmares of those who are responsible. I know she will haunt mine.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240919ek9j001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
