\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Why Republicans are raising double the money in down-ballot races\b0\par\par\uc2 Joan E Greve \par \uc2 1,295 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2024\par 15:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans maintain a cash advantage over Democrats in one key area: state legislative races. This resource gap is now rearing its head in battleground states\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  launched her presidential bid in July, Democrats have showered her campaign with cash. Last month alone, the vice-president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/sep/06/trump-harris-election-fundraising"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raised}}}\uc2  $361m, tripling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s fundraising haul of $130m for the month. According to Harris\u8217\'20\'19s campaign, she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/2024/09/03/harris-25-million-transfer/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought}}}\uc2  in $540m in the six weeks after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  withdrew from the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Democratic congressional candidates appear to be benefiting from this financial windfall as well, as Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2024/09/03/republican-campaign-money-problems-congress-00176947"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sound the alarm}}}\uc2  about their fundraising deficit in key races that will determine control of the House and Senate in November.\par \par \uc2 But in one crucial area, Republicans maintain a substantial cash advantage over Democrats: state legislative races. In recent years, Republicans have controlled more state legislative chambers than Democrats, giving them more power over those states\u8217\'20\'19 budgets, election laws and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee (DLCC), which supports the party\u8217\'20\'19s state legislative candidates, has raised $35m between the start of 2023 and the end of this June, the committee told the Guardian. In comparison, the Republican State Leadership Committee (RSLC) \u8211\'20\'13 which invests in an array of state-level campaigns, such as supreme court races, in addition to legislative campaigns \u8211\'20\'13 has raised $62m in the same time period.\par \par \uc2 That resource gap is now rearing its head in key battleground states, the DLCC says. In Pennsylvania, a crucial state for the presidential and congressional maps, Republican state legislative candidates have spent $4.5m on paid advertisements, compared with $1.4m for Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we think about the context of what\u8217\'20\'19s at stake, we think about more than 65 million people being covered by our target map this year,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heather Williams, president of the DLCC. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that means that the rights of all those people will be determined by who\u8217\'20\'19s in power the day after the election.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A race to fill the funding gap\par \par \uc2 Democratic party leaders seem aware of the high-stakes surrounding state legislative races. Earlier this month, the Harris campaign and the \uc2 Democratic National Committee{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/2024/09/03/harris-25-million-transfer/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  a transfer of $25m in funds to help down-ballot candidates, including $2.5m for the DLCC. Williams said the transfer represented the party\u8217\'20\'19s largest investment to date focused solely on winning state legislative chambers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe underlying story here is that the Harris campaign, our federal officials [and] the party believe that we need to win up and down the ballot,\u8221\'20\'1D Williams said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that our freedoms are on the line, that democracy is on the line in the states, and so investing in state legislatures is really an emerging cornerstone of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 strategy to protect against Maga [\u8216\'20\'18Make America great again\u8217\'20\'19] extremism.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the $2.5m investment, while significant, does not come close to closing with DLCC\u8217\'20\'19s resource deficit against the RSLC. Democratic-aligned outside groups, such as the States Project and the Super Pac Forward Majority, are trying to help close that gap: Forward Majority is now on track to spend $45m this election cycle on promoting Democratic state legislative campaigns, the group announced on Wednesday. The States Project has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/12/us/politics/democrats-states-project.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  plans to spend $70m this cycle, after investing heavily in state legislative races two years ago.\par \par \uc2 This level of the ballot is critically important\par \par \uc2 Leslie Martes, Forward Majority\par \par \uc2 Forward Majority formed in 2017, after Republicans notched significant victories up and down the ballot in 2016. At the time, Democrats controlled 31 legislative chambers compared with Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 68. In the years since, Democrats have chipped away at that disadvantage, now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncsl.org/about-state-legislatures/state-partisan-composition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 controlling}}}\uc2  39 chambers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was incredible in that decade or so to see people pay more attention to it, to recognize this level of the ballot is critically important,\u8221\'20\'1D said Leslie Martes, chief strategy officer at Forward Majority.\par \par \uc2 Despite that progress, Martes warned against complacency, as Republican-aligned groups have shown a willingness to invest heavily in state legislative races as well. During the legislative races in Virginia last year, the conservative Pac \uc2 Americans for Prosperity{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.followthemoney.org/show-me?dt=2&is-s=VA&is-y=2023&f-fc=2#[%7B1%7Cgro=is-f-eid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent}}}\uc2  $2.2m, which represented the largest contribution of any outside group.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t let the Republicans flood the zone with late money,\u8221\'20\'1D Martes said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have access to it, and we can never underestimate their ability to come in late.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Post-Roe momentum\par \par \uc2 Democrats had a good year in 2022 when it came to state legislative elections. They flipped the Minnesota senate and both chambers of the Michigan legislature, giving them governing trifectas in both states thanks to the Democratic governors there. Some of that momentum was attributed to Democratic voters\u8217\'20\'19 increased focus on state legislatures after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 Of the 22 states that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently enforce}}}\uc2  an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban of some kind, 19 of them are fully controlled by Republicans. (A judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/12/north-dakota-abortion-ban-judge-strikes-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck down}}}\uc2  North Dakota\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on Thursday, but the law had not yet been enjoined as of Friday afternoon.) Meanwhile, several states controlled by Democrats \u8211\'20\'13 including California, Minnesota and New Jersey \u8211\'20\'13 have expanded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since Roe was overturned. That contrast has enlightened many voters on the importance of state elections, Martes argued.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think most people did not believe that Roe was going to fall,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I think it\u8217\'20\'19s been incredibly impactful for people to now know it\u8217\'20\'19s up to the states \u8230\'20\'26 I think that\u8217\'20\'19s really helped people focus in on state legislatures.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Williams hopes that greater awareness will translate into electoral success down ballot, as Democrats look to sustain the trifectas they won in 2022 and flip more legislative chambers.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s going to take one vote at a time and one legislative win over and over in all of these states\par \par \uc2 Heather Williams, Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee\par \par \uc2 The DLCC\u8217\'20\'19s to-do list for November is long. Democrats want to keep their control of the Michigan house and the Pennsylvania house, where they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.spotlightpa.org/news/2024/02/pennsylvania-state-house-democratic-majority-special-election-bucks-county/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repeatedly defended}}}\uc2  their slim majority through several special elections since 2022. The party also hopes to break a Republican supermajority in North Carolina, where the state legislature was able to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/16/north-carolina-abortion-ban-veto-override"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 override}}}\uc2  the Democratic governor\u8217\'20\'19s veto of a 12-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. And in Arizona, Republicans have only one-seat majorities in both chambers, giving Democrats an opportunity for their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/05/28/arizona-state-elections-2024-legislative-majority-at-risk-for-gop/73700537007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first}}}\uc2  governing trifecta in the state since 1966.\par \par \uc2 It is no coincidence that much of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 state legislative map overlaps with their presidential and congressional maps, Martes said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe key to winning back Congress also runs through a path of picking up state legislative seats and protecting important incumbents,\u8221\'20\'1D Martes said.\par \par \uc2 While leaders of both political parties are fond of saying that every vote matters, the truism is particularly relevant when it comes to state legislative races. Because the voting pool is smaller compared with a congressional or presidential race, a couple hundred votes often separate the winner from the loser in state legislative elections.\par \par \uc2 In one stunning case that arose in 2018, a tied Virginia legislative election {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/republican-yancey-picked-in-random-lottery-declared-winner-of-pivotal-va-house-race/2018/01/04/9c9caa5a-f0a1-11e7-b390-a36dc3fa2842_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was decided}}}\uc2  by pulling a name out of a bowl, and the lucky Republican winner gave his party control of the house of delegates.\par \par \uc2 While the narrow margins of such races may seem daunting, they can also be motivating, Williams argued.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to take one vote at a time and one legislative win over and over in all of these states,\u8221\'20\'1D Williams said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think we\u8217\'20\'19re at such a moment where those efforts \u8211\'20\'13 seemingly small efforts \u8211\'20\'13 will make a huge difference. And we\u8217\'20\'19ll be able to protect the rights of millions of people across the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240917ek9h001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
