\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Missouri supreme court keeps ballot measure to protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 770 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par 20:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State\u8217\'20\'19s top court rules to put proposed amendment to voters after it was opposed by lower-court judge\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/missouri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  residents will vote in November on a proposed amendment to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state constitution, after the state supreme court ruled Tuesday the measure could be put to voters.\par \par \uc2 The decision marked the culmination of a heated weeks-long battle over the ballot measure, which proposes to protect people\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamental right to reproductive freedom\u8221\'20\'1D, including the right to birth control and to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until fetal viability, or about 24 weeks of pregnancy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had challenged the measure as invalid, arguing that it violated Missouri law because it did not list the existing laws that it would repeal.\par \par \uc2 The measure, they wrote in court documents, \u8220\'20\'1Cis a proposal to repeal Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that does not disclose to voters that it will repeal Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It illegally induces Missouri voters to do more than they wish to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri currently bans \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  except in medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Missourians for Constitutional Freedom, the group behind the measure, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://missouriindependent.com/2024/09/09/missouri-abortion-rights-amendment-state-supreme-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 collected more than 380,000 signatures}}}\uc2  in support of the proposed amendment. The group argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are using a \u8220\'20\'1Cnovel\u8221\'20\'1D legal theory to kick the measure off the ballot and that a new amendment would not repeal Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws but create a new one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo recorded decision has ever accepted such a theory and used it to decertify a proposed constitutional amendment that was supported by the requisite number of voters\u8217\'20\'19 signatures,\u8221\'20\'1D the group\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers wrote in court records, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CHundreds of thousands have exercised their power as citizens, and want their fellow Missourians to vote on whether to change the Constitution with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last week, a lower-court judge ruled to remove the measure but asked for a final ruling from the state supreme court. Still, on Monday, \uc2 Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s secretary of state\uc2 , \uc2 Jay Ashcroft\uc2 , decertified the measure.\par \par \uc2 In front of the supreme court on Tuesday, lawyer Charles Hatfield, who represented Missourians for Constitutional Freedom, told the judges that the decertification, although largely symbolic, represented \u8220\'20\'1Copen contempt for your authority\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s open contempt for the rule of law,\u8221\'20\'1D Hatfield continuing, punctuating his point by slamming his finger down on his podium. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s open contempt for the proper administration of justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hours after the hearing, before an evening deadline to finalize the ballot, the Missouri supreme court ruled to let voters see the measure. The one-page order did not immediately include a majority opinion to explain the court\u8217\'20\'19s reasoning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COpinions to follow,\u8221\'20\'1D read the order, which was signed by Chief Justice Mary Russell.\par \par \uc2 In the years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , voters in multiple states \u8211\'20\'13 including Michigan, Ohio and Kansas \u8211\'20\'13 have moved to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Come November, nearly a dozen states are set to vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measures, including Nevada and Arizona, two key battleground states in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 But as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have latched onto ballot measures as a way to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have launched new efforts to take them down.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/26/nebraska-restrictions-abortion-gender-healthcare-minors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nebraska}}}\uc2 , which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 12 weeks of pregnancy, is set to let its residents vote on two competing measures: one that would add the 12-week ban into the state constitution, and another that would restore the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until fetal viability. However, on Monday, the state supreme court heard arguments in lawsuits that aim to stop one or both of the measures from reaching voters, in part based on the argument that they violate state law by dealing with too many subjects.\par \par \uc2 The court is expected to rule by Friday, the deadline for the Nebraska ballot to be certified.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, a trial is set to begin on 23 September over a ballot measure that also seeks to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights up until viability. That trial, which will begin after early voting is already underway, will deal with whether the signatures collected in support of the measure are valid.\par \par \uc2 James Leach, a lawyer for Dakotans for Health, the organization behind the ballot measure, warned that the trial could be perceived by voters as a form of election interference, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://southdakotasearchlight.com/2024/09/03/abortion-ballot-measure-challenge-on-course-for-late-september-trial/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Dakota Searchlight}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re asking for something I hope makes a little shudder run through you, which is to declare that the ballot measure is invalid, and that no one\u8217\'20\'19s vote should count,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240910ek9a003bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
