\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 State  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans are forcing doctors to provide substandard care \u8211\'20\'13 new study\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Research group describes health workers waiting until patients \u8216\'20\'18on brink of death\u8217\'20\'19 before providing care\par \par \uc2 More than two years after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are forcing doctors to provide substandard medical care, new research released Monday shows.\par \par \uc2 The study describes how one woman, whose water broke too early on her pregnancy, ended up in the ICU with severe sepsis because she could not get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end her doomed pregnancy. Her story is one of dozens of narratives collected by the research group Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health, which is housed at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another woman\u8217\'20\'19s liver transplant was canceled because doctors discovered that she was pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 even though the pregnancy was unwanted. Yet another was forced to give birth to a baby with anencephaly, a severe and fatal fetal anomaly. By the time the baby was handed over to the mother, the baby\u8217\'20\'19s skin had turned from pink to navy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe scream and wailing she let out once she saw the baby was soul-crushing and enough to break everyone in the room,\u8221\'20\'1D a medical student recounted to researchers. \u8220\'20\'1CThe mother kept screaming \u8216\'20\'18Why, God?\u8217\'20\'19 in Spanish over and over, but this was not a problem up to the divine, but rather a completely man-made problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health first started collecting narratives from doctors about their post-Roe experiences in August 2022, shortly after Roe fell in the supreme court case Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization. The group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/sites/default/files/2023-05/Care%20Post-Roe%20Preliminary%20Findings.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published a preliminary report}}}\uc2  of their findings in May 2023; they have collected a total of 86 narratives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne question that we had was: were the changes that we were seeing initially related to the initial shock of the Dobbs decisions and the laws that then went into effect? Would clinical protocols kind of adjust and healthcare providers would figure out how to provide high-quality care? Would these poor-quality cases disappear?\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr \uc2 Daniel Grossman\uc2 , the lead author on the study. \u8220\'20\'1CThese cases are continuing to happen.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, more than a dozen states have totally banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Every state with a ban on the books technically allows abortions in cases of medical emergencies, but the exact details of what constitutes an \u8220\'20\'1Cemergency\u8221\'20\'1D can differ from state to state. Doctors across the country have said that exceptions in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are worded so vaguely and confusingly that they are unworkable in practice.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, if doctors realize a patient is careening towards a medical emergency, they act as swiftly as possible. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, doctors have said, force them to wait to act until patients are on the brink of death.\par \par \uc2 Some patients experienced massive, life-threatening blood loss after being delayed care for miscarriages, according to the Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health report. One doctor in a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban told researchers that staffers at a hospital declined to help a woman who was in severe pain in the midst of a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will never forget this case because I overheard the primary provider say to a nurse that so much as offering a helping hand to a patient getting onto the gurney while in the throes of a miscarriage could be construed as \u8216\'20\'18aiding and abetting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19. Best not to so much as touch the patient who is miscarrying,\u8221\'20\'1D the doctor said. \u8220\'20\'1CA gross violation of common sense and the oath I took when I got into this profession to soothe my patients\u8217\'20\'19 suffering.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of the clinicians talked about wanting to move to a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still legal, even though they weren\u8217\'20\'19t primarily \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers,\u8221\'20\'1D Grossman said.\par \par \uc2 Most patients described in the report were between the ages of 18 and 35, and were dealing with second-trimester pregnancy complications, ectopic pregnancies (which are nonviable), fetal anomalies or miscarriages. Black and Latinx patients were, researchers noted, disproportionately represented; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/sites/default/files/2021-06/Baumgartner_racial_disparities_chartbook_v2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research has long shown}}}\uc2  that they are more likely to endure substandard care.\par \par \uc2 Patients who were in some way connected to the carceral system also faced obstacles to care. In one case, a 15-year-old in juvenile detention had a baby because she could not get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in her state. In another case, a woman who was on parole in a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban asked for permission to leave for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was denied. She left the state anyway for the procedure, but refused sedation because she feared being drug tested.\par \par \uc2 There is little quantitative research into how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are affecting medical care, Grossman said, although {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/24/texas-abortion-ban-infant-deaths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one study has uncovered a link}}}\uc2  between Texas\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 which was in effect for about 10 months before Dobbs ushered in a near-total ban \u8211\'20\'13 and an increase in infant deaths.\par \par \uc2 Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health continues to receive reports from doctors, Grossman said, although the number of submissions has declined.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think part of the reason that the number of submissions has gone down is that clinicians have become kind of inured to this,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis has now become the new normal, and it\u8217\'20\'19s no longer as shocking. They\u8217\'20\'19re also just really burnt out and frustrated by not being able to provide the care that they were trained to provide.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240909ek990018l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
