\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Red Dawn conservatives\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18Dobbs dads\u8217\'20\'19: anti-Trump groups aim to peel off voters\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2024\par 13:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats target key group: conservative men who don\u8217\'20\'19t want \u8216\'20\'18big government\u8217\'20\'19 attacking their daughters\u8217\'20\'19 rights\par \par \uc2 A siren blares. Feet crunch on gravel. A county sheriff looks into a car and tells a teenage girl he knows she is pregnant. He arrests her father for driving her to a state where she can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd you, young lady,\u8221\'20\'1D the sheriff says, \u8220\'20\'1Cwell, you\u8217\'20\'19re under arrest for evading motherhood.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3FGIyxhGkvo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an advert from the Lincoln Project}}}\uc2 , a pro-democracy group, presenting a dark vision of the future for millions of American women if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  defeats \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in the presidential election and criminalises \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 But its target audience includes another crucial group of voters: conservative men.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDobbs Dads\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 named after the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs decision, which in 2022 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/10/divided-america-roe-v-wade-supreme-court-cold-civil-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ended the constitutional right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 are suburban gen X and millennial fathers angered by the prospect of \u8220\'20\'1Cbig government\u8221\'20\'1D making choices for their wives\u8217\'20\'19 and daughters\u8217\'20\'19 reproductive healthcare needs.\par \par \uc2 The group has been identified as crucial by the Lincoln Project\u8217\'20\'19s sister organisation, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lincolndemocracyinstitute.us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lincoln Democracy Institute}}}\uc2 , along with \u8220\'20\'1CRed Dawn conservatives\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 inspired by the 1984 film Red Dawn, in which American teenagers fight invading Soviet forces \u8211\'20\'13 who value a strong national defence and traditional alliances.\par \par \uc2 It is the latest attempt to find a snappy shorthand to describe the type of voters whose decisions are set to affect the whole of America and the world. That is because, in the absence of a national popular vote, the result of the presidential election is likely to come down to just tens of thousands of votes in seven swing states.\par \par \uc2 The first iteration was \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1996/10/06/us/suburbs-soccer-moms-fleeing-the-gop-are-much-sought.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soccer moms}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D backing the Democrat \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  against the Republican \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2  in 1996. But after the terrorist attacks on New York and Washington on 11 September 2001, the then senator \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,1004271,00.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opined}}}\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Csoccer moms are security moms now\u8221\'20\'1D as the Republican \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  sought re-election.\par \par \uc2 Later elections delivered the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/BL-WB-45531"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStarbucks mom\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.startribune.com/in-pursuit-of-undecided-waitress-mom-voters/175713421/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cwaitress mom\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/politics/2012/08/15/walmart-moms-seen-as/23988164007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWalmart mom\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , labels generally applied to suburban white women facing economic struggles. Then there \u8220\'20\'1CNascar dads\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 referring to mostly white, middle-aged, working-class or lower-middle-class men \u8211\'20\'13 and \u8220\'20\'1Cmama bears\u8221\'20\'1D, conservative mothers and grandmothers who in recent years have organised for parental rights.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re disproportionately girl dads... Their conservatism is grounded in, \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t want government telling me what to do\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Trygve Olson\par \par \uc2 Now America faces another close election. The latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://emersoncollegepolling.com/august-2024-swing-state-polls-toss-up-presidential-election-in-swing-states/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 swing state surveys}}}\uc2  by Emerson College Polling and the Hill found Harris had a slight edge over Trump in Michigan, Georgia and Nevada and the candidates tied in Pennsylvania. Trump was narrowly ahead in Wisconsin, North Carolina and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 That the Dobbs decision, which overturned half a century of legal precedent protecting the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Roe v Wade, already galvanised progressive female voters in the 2022 midterm elections is no secret. But the Lincoln Democracy Institute believes that conservative men could also be crucial.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lincolnproject.us/bios/trygve-olson/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trygve Olson}}}\uc2 , a senior adviser at the Lincoln Democracy Institute, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe Dobbs dads tend to be millennial. They are more likely to be college-educated white males and they\u8217\'20\'19re disproportionately girl dads. Some of them are pro-life; they\u8217\'20\'19re conservatives. Their conservatism is grounded in, \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t want government telling me what to do.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Olson characterised the thinking of many such voters as: \u8220\'20\'1CI would hope my daughter\u8217\'20\'19s never having to face that choice but, if they were, I don\u8217\'20\'19t want some theocrat or government official making that decision. I would hope that my daughter would come to me. I\u8217\'20\'19m trying to raise her with integrity. I view my role as protector. I don\u8217\'20\'19t want government making that decision any more than I want the government telling me what I can grow on my land or what kind of shotgun I can own.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1CState Line\u8221\'20\'1D ad, warning of a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/05/project-2025-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , a radical policy blueprint from a rightwing thinktank, racked up half a million views on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  in two days. Olson, who grew up in the battleground state of Wisconsin, continued: \u8220\'20\'1C Being a girl dad kind of changes your perspective because you can empathise with a lot of things they go through.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs I say to my 15-year-old, I can empathise with some of the challenges you have and I can relate to them, but I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what it\u8217\'20\'19s like to be a 15-year-old girl. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never been one. But I do know that I can take what I know and apply it to you and that seems to be happening with these voters. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had fallen off with them. A lot of them were undecided. What we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing in the data is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has recaptured a lot. There\u8217\'20\'19s still work to be done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved a winning issue for Democrats in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/11/abortion-midterm-elections-republicans-women-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elections in 2022 and 2023}}}\uc2 . Sensing the political danger, Trump has sought to assure voters he would not seek a national ban and to distance himself from Project 2025. Olson observed: \u8220\'20\'1CYou see Trump trying to back away from Dobbs. It\u8217\'20\'19s an understanding that those voters are the ones that are going to decide these key states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Democrats seized on the issue and the theme of \u8220\'20\'1Cfreedom\u8221\'20\'1D at their recent convention in Chicago. Among the speakers was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/20/trump-abortion-ban-zurawski-response"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amanda Zurawski}}}\uc2 , who recalled going into premature labour at 18 weeks of pregnancy but being sent home by a hospital in Texas, which has an almost total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, because she was deemed not to qualify for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the law\u8217\'20\'19s exception for life-threatening emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Standing alongside her on stage, Zurawski\u8217\'20\'19s husband, Josh, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/DouglasEmhoff/status/1829183996682453469"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the convention}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m here tonight because the fight for reproductive rights isn\u8217\'20\'19t just a woman\u8217\'20\'19s fight. This is about fighting for our families and, as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says, our future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking on a \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  thinktank {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/events/takeaways-from-the-democratic-national-convention/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 panel earlier this week}}}\uc2 , Elaine Kamarck, a former White House official, commented: \u8220\'20\'1CSomebody asked me a couple of weeks ago, can you summarise \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s message in one sentence? I said, no, I can\u8217\'20\'19t, I can summarise it in one word, and the word is freedom.\par \par \uc2 What the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision did was way beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was about fundamental freedoms\par \par \uc2 Elaine Kamarck\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat is behind that? The Dobbs decision. What\u8217\'20\'19s behind that is the unprecedented decision of the supreme court to take away a half-century-old right from the population, and the population that cares about it most happens to be 55% of the electorate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kamarck added: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision did was way beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was about fundamental freedoms. Can the government come into your bedroom or into your home or into your doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office and make decisions for you? That\u8217\'20\'19s scary. It\u8217\'20\'19s particularly scary to a country, to an electorate, that was built on freedom, so here we had a situation where the Democrats took away from the Republicans an issue that used to work for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The convention also worked hard to present Harris as a viable commander-in-chief in contrast to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s isolationism, chaotic style and past derogatory statements about the former senator \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , a prisoner of war in Vietnam, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/16/trump-presidential-medal-military-honor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and other military veterans}}}\uc2 . Olson believes that \u8220\'20\'1CRed Dawn conservatives\u8221\'20\'1D could also help swing the election.\par \par \uc2 Olson said: \u8220\'20\'1CThey tend to be both male and female. They\u8217\'20\'19re gen X or late baby boomers. They\u8217\'20\'19re conservative, came of age during Reagan and Thatcher. About half of them voted for Trump. The other half didn\u8217\'20\'19t vote for Biden; they just kind of sat it out. That said, there are things that can move them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump saying [Russian leader Vladimir] Putin\u8217\'20\'19s a genius for invading Ukraine \u8211\'20\'13 they\u8217\'20\'19re very troubled by that. They\u8217\'20\'19re troubled by the Republican party no longer being that party that understood there was an evil empire, so foreign policy matters to them to a degree. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some of the Lincoln Project\u8217\'20\'19s most successful ads in the 2022 midterms targeted \u8220\'20\'1CRed Dawn conservatives\u8221\'20\'1D in battleground states, focusing on how Trump was siding with Putin against Ukraine. \u8220\'20\'1CFrom an electoral standpoint, it isn\u8217\'20\'19t about probably getting them to vote for Harris as much as it is to get them to disqualify Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of them will never pull the lever for a Democrat and probably down ballot vote Republican. But the question is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because it\u8217\'20\'19s another thing that makes them uncomfortable with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Republican primary elections showed a small but significant minority refusing to embrace Trump and his \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica first\u8221\'20\'1D foreign policy. Although {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/06/nikki-haley-drops-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she dropped out}}}\uc2  of the primaries in early March, his rival \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , espousing more traditionally hawkish positions, continued to draw up to 20% in the contests.\par \par \uc2 Harris and Trump have spent the past week travelling in swing states such as Georgia, Michigan and Wisconsin. But with the key electoral battlegrounds ranging from the midwest to the Sun belt, and spanning various demographics of age, gender and race, sorting voters into categories \u8211\'20\'13 and finding the right message for the right audience \u8211\'20\'13 is complex.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on a panel organised by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://politics.uchicago.edu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 University of Chicago\u8217\'20\'19s Institute of Politics}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cook Political Report}}}\uc2  on the sidelines of the Democratic convention, the pollster {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/JohnAnzo?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Anzalone}}}\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhere I do think the battleground is is suburban women. What\u8217\'20\'19s key is to break the myth of what suburban women look like.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne is they\u8217\'20\'19re super diverse. It used to be in 1990 that 85% of suburban women were white. Now it\u8217\'20\'19s 61% or something like that, so it\u8217\'20\'19s diverse. It\u8217\'20\'19s older, which I think is good for Kamala in some ways as well on issue-based stuff. It\u8217\'20\'19s a mix of people who have children, who don\u8217\'20\'19t have children, etc. But why that universe becomes important is that [Hillary] Clinton got 46% of them; Biden got 56% of them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Suburban men, Anzalone noted, have edged away from Democrats. \u8220\'20\'1CSo where are you going to make that up? You can make it up with suburban women, not just because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue but because of who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240904ek91002oi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
