\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Telehealth helps drive rise in US abortions despite proliferation of bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par 13:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Remote consultations allow access for patients in states with severe curbs as January sees over 100,000 abortions\par \par \uc2 Abortions continue to rise across the US due to better access to care via telehealth, even as states push new restrictions and total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the wake of the supreme court decision overturning Roe v Wade, according to a new #WeCount {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://societyfp.org/research/wecount/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from the Society of Family Planning published on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, despite the increase in access for some patients, there are significant challenges for others who need reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 There were more than 100,000 abortions in the US in January 2024, the highest monthly rate since the study began in April 2022. In the first three months of 2024, there was an average of 98,990 abortions a month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIncreased access has addressed a lot of the pre-existing unmet need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was already there,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ushma Upadhyay, professor at \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , and co-chair of #WeCount, a research project collecting monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  volume data by state.\par \par \uc2 Reports like these were \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical, because if we don\u8217\'20\'19t know how many abortions are occurring, then we are subject to misinformation\u8221\'20\'1D, said Amanda Jean Stevenson, assistant professor of sociology at \uc2 University of Colorado Boulder\uc2 , who is not affiliated with the study.\par \par \uc2 After the Dobbs decision, states radically reshaped reproductive healthcare access, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/policies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  14 states}}}\uc2  instituting total bans and many others setting extreme limitations. Only nine states and the District of Columbia have no gestational bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Providers and policymakers in states where the procedure is legal responded to the crackdown on access by expanding telehealth, where patients can be seen remotely by physicians who can prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications.\par \par \uc2 Before the decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, about 5% of abortions happened through telehealth. Now, it accounts for 20% of all abortions in the US.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs pushed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to be creative in offering care, Upadhyay said, and telehealth is also cheaper, especially given the costs associated with traveling for in-person \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 transportation, lodgings, childcare, time off work, and more.\par \par \uc2 Providers also feel a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral imperative\u8221\'20\'1D to reach those people living under \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans who have no other choice, Upadhyay said.\par \par \uc2 Some states and districts have also passed laws to protect the rights of patients and providers \u8211\'20\'13 including shield laws, which protect clinicians offering telehealth care to people in other states.\par \par \uc2 More than 65,000 people living in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were able to access medication through telehealth visits between July 2023 and March 2024 because of shield laws.\par \par \uc2 Florida, which is geographically close to several states with restrictions, was one of five states with the most abortions each month from January to March this year. But the state enacted its own six-week ban on 1 May.\par \par \uc2 Researchers expect to see a decline in in-person abortions in Florida, but there will probably be an increase in telehealth abortions because of the new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Notably, not all patients are comfortable with or able to use telehealth, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are still impeding patients\u8217\'20\'19 ability to access reproductive healthcare, Upadhyay said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe there\u8217\'20\'19s still many people living in these states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans who are not getting the abortions that they desire because of all of the barriers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Stevenson also pointed out: \u8220\'20\'1CJust because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasing doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean there aren\u8217\'20\'19t still people who can\u8217\'20\'19t get abortions who want them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240807ek87001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
