\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Project 2025: what does the rightwing blueprint say about  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,387 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2024\par 16:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Policy playbook developed for potential Trump 2.0 term aims to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, increase surveillance and champion fetal personhood\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , the wishlist for a Trump 2.0 administration drawn up by the influential thinktank the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/01/kevin-roberts-trump-heritage-foundation-project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Heritage Foundation}}}\uc2 , proposes mobilizing an array of government agencies to curb access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 up to and including a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills that would affect even states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Backed by more than 100 conservative organizations, the 922-page Project 2025 has become notorious in recent weeks as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2  has started bringing it up at rallies. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/08/trump-project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump has tried to distance himself}}}\uc2  from its many proposals , claiming he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know anything about Project 2025 and has \u8220\'20\'1Cno idea who is behind it\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 even though his administration\u8217\'20\'19s officials wrote chunks of it. While the director of the blueprint stepped down last week, a move that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/01/project-2025-key-proposals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump campaign celebrated}}}\uc2  and that leaves its future operations unclear, the policy ideas endure and closely {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/09/project-2025-trump-connections-similarities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 align with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s platform}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Dobbs decision [overturning {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  ] is just the beginning,\u8221\'20\'1D Project 2025 reads. \u8220\'20\'1CConservatives in the states and in Washington, including in the next conservative administration, should push as hard as possible to protect the unborn in every jurisdiction in America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 takes direct aim at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills}}}\uc2 , indicates that fetuses should have legal rights, and seeks to expand \u8220\'20\'1Csurveillance\u8221\'20\'1D of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while eliminating government support for the procedure. In its architects\u8217\'20\'19 view, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not healthcare and should never be treated as such.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProject 2025\u8217\'20\'19s whole-of-government approach to dismantling reproductive rights by weaponizing federal agencies and consolidating executive branch power is unprecedented \u8211\'20\'13 and goes against most Americans\u8217\'20\'19 wishes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Caroline Ciccone, president of the watchdog group Accountable.US, which has been studying the plan.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the proposals that could rewrite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US:\par \par \uc2 Enforce the Comstock Act\par \par \uc2 Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s most audacious proposal may be the easiest to enact: the playbook suggests that a future conservative administration enforce the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jun/20/comstock-act-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Comstock Act}}}\uc2 , a 151-year-old anti-obscenity law that prohibits the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials, to ban people from shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics rely on the mail to send and receive the pills, as do the advocates who help people induce their own abortions, enforcing the Comstock Act could result in a de facto national ban on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After Roe fell, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s Department of Justice issued guidance saying that the Comstock Act only applies to people who intentionally break the law. But a Trump administration could roll back that guidance and thus sidestep a gridlocked Congress to ban the most popular type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reverse \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills\par \par \uc2 This year, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  rejected a lawsuit from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who wanted to reverse the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mifepristone}}}\uc2 , a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, ruling unanimously that the activists did not have the legal standing to sue. But a Project 2025 proposal suggests avoiding the courts altogether and, instead, having the \uc2 FDA\uc2  reverse its 2000 approval of mifepristone altogether. That reversal would yank the drug from the market.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow that the supreme court has acknowledged that the constitution contains no right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is ethically and legally obliged to revisit and withdraw its initial approval,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2 , who served as the head of \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  (\uc2 HHS\uc2 ) office of civil rights under Trump, wrote in Project 2025. Severino also suggests that, as an \u8220\'20\'1Cinterim step\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  reverse guidance that allows providers to offer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/24/hawaii-mifepristone-abortion-pill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abortions via telemedicine}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which currently accounts for 19% of all abortions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 FDA\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t a purely independent agency,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the University of California, Davis School of Law who studies the legal history of reproduction. \u8220\'20\'1CThe secretary of \uc2 HHS\uc2  has a lot of ability to override both \uc2 FDA\uc2  rule-making and \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval decisions. Even if the \uc2 FDA\uc2  does not want to do anything, the secretary of \uc2 HHS\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who\u8217\'20\'19s a political appointee \u8211\'20\'13 could or would, potentially.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Roll back privacy protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients\par \par \uc2 In June 2022, after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the Biden administration issued guidance asserting that, under the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), healthcare providers can talk to law enforcement about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients only in \u8220\'20\'1Cnarrowly tailored\u8221\'20\'1D circumstances. For example, hospital workers are not allowed to tell cops that they suspect someone may have taken pills to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Cops also cannot demand records from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic without a court order.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 demands that the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  withdraw that guidance. \u8220\'20\'1CHIPAA covers patients in the womb, but this guidance treats them as nonpersons contrary to law,\u8221\'20\'1D the playbook reads. \u8220\'20\'1CThe guidance is unnecessary and contributes to ideologically motivated fearmongering about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Dobbs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is legal in all but one state \u8211\'20\'13 Nevada \u8211\'20\'13 to \u8220\'20\'1Cself-manage\u8221\'20\'1D your own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have long warned that, if a prosecutor wants to charge you for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they will find a statute elastic enough to do it. Between 2000 and 2020, 61 people were criminally investigated or arrested for allegedly self-managing their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or helping someone else do so.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s language also suggests that fetuses deserve to be legally recognized as people. If this idea, known as \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D, were ever fully enacted, it would not only completely ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but rewrite vast swathes of US law.\par \par \uc2 Increase CDC \u8216\'20\'18surveillance\u8217\'20\'19 over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The CDC already collects information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from most of the country, in a report that the agency titles \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/06/abortion-laws-surveillance-privacy-phone-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  surveillance}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. But its reports are incomplete, since states such as California do not supply the data. Project 2025 suggests that the CDC should go so far as to cut funds from a state if it does not tell the CDC \u8220\'20\'1Cexactly how many abortions take place within its borders, at what gestational age of the child, for what reason, the mother\u8217\'20\'19s state of residence, and by what method\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Eliminate some immigrant girls\u8217\'20\'19 access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 During the Trump administration, officials at the Office of Refugee Resettlement (ORR) \u8211\'20\'13 which cares for minors who cross the border without parents or guardians \u8211\'20\'13 refused to let teen girls get abortions until a court ordered them to do so. In light of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, Project 2025 wants ORR to once again refuse to facilitate abortions. If that refusal leads to a lawsuit, the playbook wants a conservative administration to \u8220\'20\'1Cfight to defend that policy up to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Reshape the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest family planning program\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration took a hammer to Title X, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s only federal family planning program. Clinics that receive money through Title X are tasked with providing low-cost or free family planning services \u8211\'20\'13 such as birth control and STI screenings \u8211\'20\'13 to people regardless of age, immigration status or income. These clinics are already forbidden from using Title X dollars to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, but the Trump administration in 2018 barred Title X clinics from even counseling patients about the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Rather than cave to what critics saw as a \u8220\'20\'1Cgag rule\u8221\'20\'1D, more than 1,200 clinics left the Title X network. Between 2018 and 2020, the number of Title X patients dropped by 60%.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/12/after-years-havoc-biden-harris-title-x-rule-now-effect-what-you-need-know"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration rescinded}}}\uc2  Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Title X provisions but Project 2025 suggests reviving the so-called gag rule, as well as reframing the 54-year-old program \u8220\'20\'1Caround fertility awareness and holistic family planning\u8221\'20\'1D. Fertility awareness, a birth control method that involves tracking your cycle rather than using hormonal contraception, is typically only about 75% effective in preventing pregnancy. (Separately, the playbook also suggests removing Ella, a form of emergency contraception, from the Affordable Care Act\u8217\'20\'19s contraceptive mandate.)\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 wants to require Title X clinics to provide patients with information about \u8220\'20\'1Cthe importance of marriage to family and personal well-being\u8221\'20\'1D. Ultimately, the playbook says, the \uc2 HHS\uc2  should pursue a \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily agenda\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It declares: \u8220\'20\'1CMarried men and women are the ideal, natural family structure because all children have a right to be raised by the men and women who conceived them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240805ek85002be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
