\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 More US women have tried to induce their own  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  since fall of Roe \u8211\'20\'13 report\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 874 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2024\par 15:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Roughly 7% of reproductive-aged women have attempted to induce own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from 5% before fall of Roe\par \par \uc2 Roughly 7% of w??omen of reproductive age in the US have attempted to induce their own abortions outside the formal healthcare system, a new study has found, up from 5% before Roe v Wade fell in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The study, published on Tuesday in the Jama medical journal, determined how many people reported ever \u8220\'20\'1Cself-managing\u8221\'20\'1D their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2021 and again in 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 a timeline that allowed researchers to examine how Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the supreme court case that overturned Roe, has affected self-managed abortions. People of color and LGBTQ+ people were more likely to report having ever attempted to end their own pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe think because it\u8217\'20\'19s getting more difficult to access facility-based \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lauren Ralph, the study\u8217\'20\'19s lead author and an epidemiologist at Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health, a research group at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a really important piece of the puzzle, as we try to understand the full impact of the Dobbs decision on people\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Dobbs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14 states have enacted}}}\uc2  near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. On Monday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/29/iowa-six-week-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Iowa became the fourth state}}}\uc2  to ban the procedure past about six weeks of pregnancy, before many women even know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Of the women who reported in 2023 attempting to induce their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 11% said in 2023 they had used mifepristone, one of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills typically used in a two-pill protocol, and 13.7% said they used misoprostol, the other pill, on its own. Another study, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/25/self-managed-abortions-avoid-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published in March}}}\uc2 , estimated roughly 26,000 more people in the US used pills to induce their own at-home abortions than would have done so if Roe had not fallen.\par \par \uc2 Medical experts widely agree that these pills can be used to safely self-manage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the first trimester of pregnancy, and the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  even offers a protocol on how to do it. Misoprostol can safely be used on its own \u8211\'20\'13 but often comes with more side-effects than the two-pill regimen \u8211\'20\'13 while using mifepristone alone {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aidaccess.org/en/page/450/what-if-you-take-the-mifepristone-and-then-decide-not-to-take-the"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can result in more complications}}}\uc2  and is not typically recommended when ending a pregnancy .\par \par \uc2 Far more people tried to end their pregnancies in potentially unsafe ways. In 2023, about 25% of those who attempted to self-manage their abortions used herbs, 22% hit themselves in the stomach and 19% used alcohol or other drugs. Another 15% said that, after their attempted self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they experienced a complication \u8211\'20\'13 such as bleeding or pain \u8211\'20\'13 that led them to see a doctor or nurse.\par \par \uc2 Asked why they tried to self-manage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people said they wanted privacy, that going to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic was too expensive or they preferred trying on their own first. Nearly 13% said they were concerned about clinic protesters.\par \par \uc2 Four in 10 people who have ever self-managed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were younger than 20 when they did it, and 9% of people who had tried to self-manage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were concerned about needing parental consent. Many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -tolerant states still have parental involvement laws on the books, which require minors to tell their parents about their plans to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or to get their consent for the procedure. If a minor cannot or will not do so, they have to go to court and convince a judge to let them get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without parental knowledge or consent \u8211\'20\'13 which can be a tall ask.\par \par \uc2 Ralph was not surprised to find that so many people who try to end their own pregnancies are so young.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGiven things like parental-involvement requirements, given things like difficulty accessing transportation, given things like not having your own bank accounts or having a consistent source of income in the same way that a young adult might, I do think that young people might be more likely to turn to self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than their older counterparts,\u8221\'20\'1D Ralph said. Young people\u8217\'20\'19s experiences of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she added, had long been understudied.\par \par \uc2 Because people who self-manage abortions can face criminal consequences, Ralph and her fellow researchers used statistical analyses to account for people who may not be accurately reporting their experiences with the phenomenon. Just one state \u8211\'20\'13 Nevada \u8211\'20\'13 bans self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but legal experts have long warned that if a prosecutor wants to punish someone for attempting one, they will find a statute pliable enough to do it. Earlier this year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/11/ohio-woman-miscarriage-not-indicted-abuse-corpse-brittany-watts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an Ohio grand jury considered whether}}}\uc2  to indict a woman accused of abusing a corpse after she miscarried into a toilet. (It ultimately declined to charge her.)\par \par \uc2 Ralph found that about a third of people in both 2021 and 2023 said that their self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeded, while a fifth said that they later underwent an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a clinic. Another quarter of people said that nothing happened and that they may never have been pregnant in the first place. Fourteen per cent continued their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240730ek7u00262\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
