\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Harris campaign centers healthcare and  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights: \u8216\'20\'18If she wins, it\u8217\'20\'19s because of Dobbs\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2024\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Vice-president emphasizes bodily autonomy and affordable care as experts expect Democrats to remain on offense\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is staking out health, and especially reproductive rights and affordable healthcare, as a central issue in her historic campaign for the US presidency, experts say.\par \par \uc2 The first ad for the Harris campaign, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/25/we-choose-freedom-kamala-harris-campaign-launches-first-ad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released on Thursday}}}\uc2 , prioritized bodily autonomy, safety from gun violence, and affordable healthcare alongside issues like child poverty and the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights will be \u8220\'20\'1Cfar and away\u8221\'20\'1D the driving focus of Harris\u8217\'20\'19s health-related messages \u8211\'20\'13 and her entire campaign \u8211\'20\'13 as well as prioritization of affordable healthcare and medications, according to \uc2 Drew Altman\uc2 , president of KFF, a nonpartisan health policy organization.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Harris wins this election, I think it would be because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and because of Dobbs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Greer Donley, associate professor at the University of Pittsburgh School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a huge issue this election cycle \u8230\'20\'26 and I think it will really help the campaign to capitalize on all of the momentum and all of the rage and anger about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Dobbs decision overturning the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Biden-Harris administration has \u8220\'20\'1Cdone an extraordinary job on this issue with the tools that they have\u8221\'20\'1D, said Sabrina Corlette, research professor at Georgetown University\u8217\'20\'19s Center on Health Insurance Reforms.\par \par \uc2 The administration solidified telehealth access for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowed veterans affairs hospitals to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  counseling and services, clarified the Hipaa privacy rules to make sure health employees don\u8217\'20\'19t give protected information to law enforcement, and made sure that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , miscarriage, stillbirth and other reproductive healthcare are entitled to accommodations under the Pregnant Workers Fairness Act.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey did a lot,\u8221\'20\'1D Donley said.\par \par \uc2 In other aspects of health, the administration has built on the Affordable Care Act and the Medicaid program to expand coverage and affordability, and began negotiating Medicare drug pricing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have seen a real improvement in the numbers of people who have health insurance, and new resources to make health insurance more affordable,\u8221\'20\'1D Corlette said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would expect a Harris administration to try to build on and expand the gains in the Affordable Care Act,\u8221\'20\'1D also known as Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 Harris\u8217\'20\'19 campaign would probably go on the offense, Altman said, calling out Trump\u8217\'20\'19s policies and proposals to cut immensely popular health programs and contrasting them with gains over the past three years.\par \par \uc2 Her posture probably \u8220\'20\'1Cwill be characterized much more by attacking Trump on positions she\u8217\'20\'19ll claim he will take on healthcare than by advancing her own healthcare positions\u8221\'20\'1D, Altman said.\par \par \uc2 As a senator, Harris was the first to co-sponsor the Medicare for All legislation put forward by the Vermont senator Bernie Sanders, and as a presidential candidate in 2020, she unveiled her own version of a similar plan.\par \par \uc2 In her time in Congress, Harris also sponsored a bill to prohibit certain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and she introduced a bill to require public and private health insurers to cover medications that prevent HIV infections.\par \par \uc2 But \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the past is going to be terribly relevant\u8221\'20\'1D, Altman said. As a presidential candidate, Harris needed to rally the base and appeal to undecided voters, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would expect her to continue the kind of aggressive incrementalism that we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen from the Biden administration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One of the first healthcare battles facing a Harris administration would be the extension of the Affordable Care Act tax credits originally enacted in 2021, now scheduled to expire at the end of 2025, Corlette said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a really important, critical battle, and I expect that she will champion a permanent extension of those \u8211\'20\'13 because that\u8217\'20\'19s just had a huge impact on the affordability of health insurance coverage for a lot of people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans have proposed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbpp.org/research/health/congressional-republicans-budget-plans-are-likely-to-cut-health-coverage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 capping and cutting Medicaid}}}\uc2 , a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery popular\u8221\'20\'1D program that covers almost 90 million Americans, Altman said. Some conservative Republican groups have also put forward fundamental changes to Medicare, which Altman calls \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitically a sacrosanct program\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Trump and Vance have proposed weakening protections for people with pre-existing conditions, which are \u8220\'20\'1Cimmensely popular\u8221\'20\'1D, Altman said, as well as major changes to or even repealing the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 Republican groups have also revived a focus on tax-preferred health savings accounts, in an effort to make healthcare more consumer-driven.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat you\u8217\'20\'19d likely see under a Trump administration is a dismantling of the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s involvement in healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D Corlette said. Yet \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the biggest pain points for Americans right now is the cost of healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 not just the cost of health insurance, but actually the cost when they go to the hospital or go to the doctor,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Trump has proposed leaving the issue to the states \u8211\'20\'13 nearly one-third of which have total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is not a position that is consistent with the majority of the American people,\u8221\'20\'1D Donley said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s radical.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plank also invokes fetal personhood, which could make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , IVF procedures, and miscarriages prosecutable as murder. The concept of fetal personhood, wherein a zygote, embryo or fetus has the same rights as people, is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery scary. It\u8217\'20\'19s scary as everyone suggests it is, and not just for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Donley said.\par \par \uc2 While Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration has made major strides on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s never been an issue that I think he personally has felt comfortable with,\u8221\'20\'1D Corlette said. Biden rarely even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/gender-identity/why-doesnt-biden-use-the-word-abortion-more/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says}}}\uc2  the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Harris, on the other hand, has been much more outspoken about reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Whoever wins this election could one day nominate new justices to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , if some of the older justices were to leave the court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Harris serves eight years, then she has the potential to really change the composition of the supreme court,\u8221\'20\'1D Donley said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat could be a method to get Dobbs overturned. And obviously that would have implications that are far bigger than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240729ek7t001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
