\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Preventable deaths and high cancer rates: women\u8217\'20\'19s health in \u8216\'20\'18perilous\u8217\'20\'19 place\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2024\par 11:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Report finds \u8216\'20\'18clear distinctions\u8217\'20\'19 in US women\u8217\'20\'19s health outcomes, which can be linked to states\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws\par \par \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s health is in a \u8220\'20\'1Cperilous\u8221\'20\'1D place with deep and growing inequalities across regions of the US, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/scorecard/2024/jul/2024-state-scorecard-womens-health-and-reproductive-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new report}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Commonwealth Fund\uc2  has found. The report ranks states based on 32 indicators, ranging from insurance coverage to breast and cervical cancer to Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts, Rhode Island and Vermont ranked at the top across a range of health issues. South-eastern states \u8211\'20\'13 most of which now have near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8211\'20\'13 ranked at the bottom on a wide range of women\u8217\'20\'19s health indicators.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere you live matters to your health and healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Joseph Betancourt, president of the \uc2 Commonwealth Fund\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThis is having a disproportionate effect on women of color and women with low incomes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The goal of the report was to contribute to policies, \u8220\'20\'1Cguaranteeing all women across the US can have access to quality affordable care \u8211\'20\'13 no matter where they live\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The report largely relies on public data from 2022, a pivotal year in women\u8217\'20\'19s health, when the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade. For nearly 50 years, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  provided a federal right to terminate a pregnancy. When the decision was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned}}}\uc2 , regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reverted to the states. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fourteen}}}\uc2  quickly issued near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including many across the south-east.\par \par \uc2 Where you live matters to your health and healthcare\par \par \uc2 Dr Joseph Betancourt, \uc2 Commonwealth Fund\uc2  president\par \par \uc2 A map of the worst-performing states in the nation looks like a belt across the American south, stretching from Nevada to Florida and north to Tennessee. Notably, Nevada does not have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but tends to have worse health metrics because of a large, first-generation immigrant population.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are clear distinctions between states and delivery systems that perform well for women,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Sara Collins\uc2 , a vice-president at Commonwealth and senior scholar studying healthcare access and quality.\par \par \uc2 States that tended to perform worst had not expanded {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/medicaid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Medicaid}}}\uc2 , a public health insurance for low-income Americans. They also tended to have fewer healthcare providers overall, particularly OB-GYNs. Those regional differences are likely to be exacerbated by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, which make it legally perilous for doctors to practice.\par \par \uc2 Deaths from all causes were highest in south-eastern states \u8211\'20\'13 including from preventable causes such as substance use, Covid-19, pregnancy-related deaths and chronic treatable conditions.\par \par \uc2 Poor performing states also tended to miss opportunities to identify and treat at-risk women. They screened fewer women for postpartum depression, had higher rates of syphilis and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2023/oct/17/congenital-syphilis-crisis-us-biden-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 congenital syphilis}}}\uc2  (when the disease is passed to a baby) and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/jan/17/us-cancer-death-rate-colorectal-cervical-breast-younger-americans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 higher rates of cancer}}}\uc2 . They had higher rates of cesarean births for low-risk pregnancies and the worst maternal mortality rates, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/article/2024/jun/05/maternal-mortality-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 particularly stark disparities}}}\uc2  between Black and white women.\par \par \uc2 Also notable is how these trends have become more pronounced in the last four decades. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/news/press-releases/us-disparities-in-health-not-inevitable/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other reports}}}\uc2 , researchers have found health disparities shrank between the 1960s and 1980s. Now, worse health outcomes in the south form what some researchers call the \u8220\'20\'1Cmultiples\u8221\'20\'1D belt, where a laundry list of health outcomes are worse \u8211\'20\'13 from cancer to diabetes to HIV infection rates.\par \par \uc2 Still, the prospects of state legislatures or Congress finding common ground to improve women\u8217\'20\'19s health look dim. Many of the issues most impactful to women\u8217\'20\'19s health are also the most politically contentious.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Republicans have actively {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/05/abortion-opposition-focus-white-evangelical-anger"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sought \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans for decades}}}\uc2  and opposed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2017/mar/13/budget-office-republican-healthcare-coverage-deficit-costs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expansion of Medicaid}}}\uc2  since the Obama administration. Republicans have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jul/23/republicans-public-health-agencies-alec-coronavirus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 undermined public health authorities}}}\uc2 , the sort of workers who are key to disrupting transmission of diseases such as syphilis.\par \par \uc2 Some researchers, such as the health equity expert \uc2 Daniel Dawes\uc2 , have described these outcomes as the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://satcherinstitute.org/priorities/political-determinants-of-health/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 political determinants of health}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 in other words, the ways policy shapes the health and wellbeing of the population.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you think of the structural conditions that many folks find themselves in, there is an underlying policy \u8211\'20\'13 political action or inaction \u8211\'20\'13 that has determined those conditions and structured the resources that has enabled those conditions to exist,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dawes, founding dean of the School of Global Health at \uc2 Meharry Medical College\uc2  in Nashville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Without addressing those politics, Dawes said, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou\u8217\'20\'19re only going to continue nibbling around the edges of the problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240720ek7k001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
