\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Australian  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  provider says new \uc2 Google\uc2  policy will make it harder to advertise services\b0\par\par\uc2 Josh Taylor \par \uc2 609 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2024\par 02:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tech firm will require global telemedicine services to register with Portland, Oregon-based certifications company LegitScriptFollow our Australia news live blog for latest updatesGet our morning and afternoon news emails, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception provider MSI says \uc2 Google\uc2  is forcing the organisation to pay thousands of dollars a year to a third-party US company to keep advertising its telehealth services in \uc2 Google\uc2  search.\par \par \uc2 Under a new health and medicines policy introduced by \uc2 Google\uc2  in May, providers offering telemedicine services globally must be certified and comply with local laws. From August providers in Australia will be required to register with the Portland, Oregon-based certifications provider LegitScript to keep advertising their services.\par \par \uc2 MSI said it would cost more than $4,700 in registration and annual fees but the price may vary. These fees are separate to the costs of advertising the services.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up for Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s free morning and afternoon email newsletters for your daily news roundup}}}\par \par \uc2 The organisation has argued that forcing healthcare providers into expensive third-party verification with a US-based company ignores the regulations and inspections under Australian state and federal governments that MSI is subject to. It argued this puts an extra hurdle up in getting advertisements online.\par \par \uc2 The managing director of MSI Australia, Greg Johnson, said access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception services would be threatened if MSI could not advertise telehealth services, particularly to those based in regional areas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe already have extensive consumer protections in place through Australian regulatory bodies and quality assurance programs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s new policy just adds an expensive, opaque layer of red tape and will ultimately cause harm to people who need care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 MSI\u8217\'20\'19s director of clinical excellence, Dr Catriona Melville, said it was concerned the US-based company was making decisions influenced by the US political climate surrounding reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe that Australian healthcare policy should be determined by Australians, not by the political landscape of another country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Google\uc2  said the policy covered all telemedicine ads and was not specific to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis helps ensure that people can safely access these services, while complying with relevant laws and industry standards,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CMany telemedicine providers in Australia and around the world have completed this certification process and are successfully running ads on our platforms.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson said LegitScript was a globally recognised accreditation body used by companies around the world, and \uc2 Google\uc2  relied on LegitScript\u8217\'20\'19s expertise to ensure user safety and advertiser compliance. There were no exceptions to the policy, the spokesperson said, but added \uc2 Google\uc2  was working with MSI to clarify the policy and requirements.\par \par \uc2 LegitScript\u8217\'20\'19s senior communications manager, David Khalaf, said the company\u8217\'20\'19s goal is to make the internet safer and more transparent by protecting consumers from the online sale of fraud and violative products.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy partnering with companies including \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and TikTok, our Healthcare Merchant Certification program ensures that pharmacies and telemedicine companies advertising on these platforms have demonstrated compliance and are operating within all laws and regulations of their applicable jurisdiction,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 MSI has previously experienced issues with advertising its services in Australia through \uc2 Google\uc2  in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/jun/27/abortion-clinic-ads-blocked-in-australian-search-results-fixed-google-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2019}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/dec/15/australian-abortion-and-contraceptive-providers-ads-banned-by-google"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  2022}}}\uc2  over the wording of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2024/mar/27/meta-and-google-accused-of-restricting-reproductive-health-information"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MSI Ghana also reported in March}}}\uc2  that its use of the term \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnancy options\u8221\'20\'1D had fallen afoul of \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ad policy in the country. \uc2 Google\uc2  denied this, arguing that the ads may have been rejected for targeting people based on sensitive health categories such as pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240718ek7i0025w\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\br